Operation Clandestine Storm works as a Pantheon thriller. In the real world, weather balloons carry instruments that measure the atmosphere. Cloud seeding can influence precipitation under some conditions, but there’s no demonstrated way to use balloons to summon or steer a severe storm. 

In the fictional version, I’d make the storm literal and leave its cause unresolved. A coastal meteorologist finds the words Operation Clandestine Storm in a sealed file dated three days before a flood. Inside is a handwritten list of streets, in the exact order they went underwater. There’s one street left on the list. It’s uphill.

That takes your idea about the dark of the senses into a new domain. The storm is visible. Its cause is in the dark. Every new clue could mean someone made it happen, someone saw it coming, or someone wanted those two things to look the same.